WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.200
Dig Dug 2, Trouble in Paradise.

00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:26.400
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:28.380
I'm Mike. And I'm Sean.

00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:32.100
Sean, it's just us today, which is fine.

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:34.260
That's not Trouble in Paradise, right?

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:35.880
Joe's still gonna come back.

00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:38.660
He's lost in Paradise.

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:40.180
Oh, okay, I gotcha.

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:41.640
Yeah, we'll get there.

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:47.120
And, you know, speaking of Trouble in Paradise, Dig Dug 2 presents the idea that,

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:54.580
you know, I don't know, like, all islands are Paradise because every map that you play on in this game is on an island,

00:00:54.580 --> 00:00:56.320
and that's where the trouble is happening.

00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:57.340
Oh, man.

00:00:57.400 --> 00:00:58.580
Wow, I did not.

00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:00.300
I did not put that together.

00:01:00.740 --> 00:01:04.120
Yeah, and I'm thinking, like, you know, these guys never visited Long Island.

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:05.820
No. Because that's not Paradise.

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:07.680
Or Three Mile Island.

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:10.020
I don't even know what Three Mile Island is.

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:14.600
Oh, that was a place in Pennsylvania where we had a nuclear meltdown.

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:17.320
Whoa. Yeah, you should read up on it.

00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:26.960
It's pretty fun. Also, we played Dig Dug 1 for the Nostalgia Bites podcast, which is, like, a Patreon-only thing.

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:32.200
So we have, like, a weird thing where some of our listeners, the ones who are nice enough to give us money,

00:01:33.020 --> 00:01:42.060
they know a little more about, like, what we think of Dig Dug than, you know, than the guys who are coming in cold here and just experiencing Dig Dug 2.

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:44.100
Two questions to follow up on that.

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:49.840
One, is it weird that they wouldn't just port Dig Dug 1 over when we've gotten so many other arcade ports?

00:01:50.140 --> 00:01:58.760
And follow up on that, do you feel like the fan base who gets Dig Dug 2 is missing anything by not getting Dig Dug 1 first?

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:06.120
I think we actually brought it up in the episode that we found it strange that Dig Dug wasn't released.

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:13.340
I mean, because it was an early game, there wasn't, there aren't too many of those launch titles, and it could have just as easily been one.

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:22.880
But, I mean, compared to this game, there's enough of a difference where I would say if they'd never played Dig Dug,

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:29.000
then they might be missing out by not hearing us talk about the first one.

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:35.500
Yeah, that's a good point, because if you only played this one, or if you played the first one,

00:02:35.660 --> 00:02:41.540
I don't think you're expecting this, especially in 2022 where you've seen Dig Dug imagery.

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:50.500
I've never seen this version of Dig Dug before, which is just a, it's funny, it's like only Dig Dug in name and inflation.

00:02:51.460 --> 00:03:06.740
Yeah, I was, I was thinking about that where, like, usually it takes several iterations or several versions of some kind of game or property for the name not to mean anything anymore,

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:09.720
but it only took this franchise one.

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:13.440
And I don't know what we're digging for anymore now.

00:03:13.660 --> 00:03:21.160
Like, Dig Dug made sense when you were digging underground and getting rid of these monsters and clearing out the way for things.

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:22.580
Now it's like...

00:03:23.020 --> 00:03:26.480
Demolition Man. Well, I was guessing, yeah, like, maybe they dug out of the ground.

00:03:26.620 --> 00:03:29.960
Maybe Dig Dug was the first game, and this is the Dugging part.

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:33.440
But the thing that this game...

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:36.740
Okay, so, should we go over, like, the premise?

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:47.740
Yes, please. Okay, so, as opposed to in the original Dig Dug, where you are just, like, a lowly excavator with some kind of inflation device to fight monsters,

00:03:47.740 --> 00:03:57.380
you are now tasked with destroying tiny islands that are infested with the same enemies that we had in the first game.

00:03:58.360 --> 00:04:06.280
But now you're destroying the island, not so much digging out the island, by, like, not really planting charges.

00:04:06.280 --> 00:04:11.540
You're just jackhammering fault lines, and then the island just falls into the ocean.

00:04:12.620 --> 00:04:14.860
Which reminds me of...

00:04:14.860 --> 00:04:17.520
I forgot who it was, but it was some...

00:04:18.520 --> 00:04:25.660
It was some, like, representative in Congress in the 90s who thought that, like...

00:04:25.660 --> 00:04:30.580
That an island could capsize if something, like, was too heavy.

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:36.060
And it just, like, displayed a very strange understanding of what islands and land masses were.

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:37.160
He must have played Dig Dug, too.

00:04:37.380 --> 00:04:40.640
Yeah, I think this might have been where he got that preconception.

00:04:42.920 --> 00:04:49.180
And I think you did a great job describing the game, but I feel like more emphasis needs to be put on these fault lines,

00:04:49.180 --> 00:04:56.660
because that's ultimately, like, that's the hook of the game, is that there are these predetermined spots on every island.

00:04:56.660 --> 00:04:57.780
And every island isn't the same.

00:04:57.880 --> 00:04:59.880
It's not just, like, a giant square or a rectangle.

00:04:59.880 --> 00:05:01.380
It's, like, unique shapes.

00:05:01.400 --> 00:05:02.960
But there are these fault lines that are placed.

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:07.760
And in a way, you know, there's not much user experimentation.

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:17.560
But there are, because they carefully place these, there is a chance to get incredibly high bonuses if you strike the lines in the right connection.

00:05:17.560 --> 00:05:21.080
So this way, a large chunk of the island falls off.

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:27.160
It is possible for you to also fall off the island, but that's the risk and reward system of this game.

00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:35.860
Yeah, I mean, there are kind of puzzles in that way where there is sort of, like, an optimal cutting route.

00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:45.340
But you also have to be mindful of where the enemies are and make sure that, like, they're not on your chunk that isn't going to fall off.

00:05:45.460 --> 00:05:50.080
Or just trying to optimize the amount of enemies that you're going to get rid of at once.

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:59.000
And it can be really satisfying when you get, like, a nice combo of, like, oh, everybody fell off and I'm fine.

00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:02.220
In the beginning of the game, it's very obvious, right?

00:06:02.360 --> 00:06:05.080
Like, they're almost spelling it out for you how the fault lines work.

00:06:05.080 --> 00:06:06.820
And, like, most of it's already connected.

00:06:06.820 --> 00:06:09.300
And you just have to finish the one line, right?

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:16.000
But later on in the later rounds, it's almost like no lines are connected yet or even, like, made.

00:06:16.040 --> 00:06:19.380
And you just have to figure out where the poles are and kind of put it together.

00:06:19.380 --> 00:06:21.680
And I'm not saying it's the hardest thing in the world.

00:06:21.720 --> 00:06:37.160
But it's definitely one of those things where if you don't pick up on the concept, the later part of this game could involve a lot more of you just inflating enemies and blowing them up to death rather than separating land masses to score maximum points.

00:06:38.260 --> 00:06:50.980
Yeah, and I guess, like, something could be said about whether the learning curve or, like, whether that's too steep or too easy.

00:06:50.980 --> 00:06:54.060
But I think that they do give you an opportunity to learn.

00:06:55.500 --> 00:07:02.300
But, yeah, like, I guess, do you think that it's too fast, like, going from easy to hard?

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:03.760
I think it's fine.

00:07:04.420 --> 00:07:07.520
Even if it is, like, you know, I mean, I don't know how many rounds are actually in this game.

00:07:07.640 --> 00:07:13.920
But let's say even after the 10th round, if it starts to get a little more difficult, I feel like that's fine for these kinds of arcade games.

00:07:13.920 --> 00:07:24.540
And that's, like, again, that's the whole risk-reward thing, right, is that if you spend too much time creating fault lines that don't lead to anything, the enemies are going to start to, you know, close in on you.

00:07:24.560 --> 00:07:26.880
And then you weren't focused on taking them out.

00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:39.060
So it's just I feel that it could be one of those things that if you don't play this game long enough, like if you just focus on killing enemies, you can probably get through all the rounds.

00:07:39.060 --> 00:07:43.220
And then it comes down to another thing that, you know, nostalgia always hates on.

00:07:43.520 --> 00:07:45.240
It comes down to just score, right?

00:07:45.340 --> 00:07:55.040
That theoretically no enemies in this game are so unstoppable that you would be trapped or unable to solve a puzzle.

00:07:55.200 --> 00:08:00.300
This is still an action game in some regards where you are in control of your own destiny.

00:08:00.300 --> 00:08:01.720
It's not a puzzle-solving game.

00:08:01.980 --> 00:08:07.820
So there's a lot going on here where it's like, I don't know, the skill level matters but only really for points.

00:08:08.200 --> 00:08:23.680
Yeah. Um, because there is enough of like a stun lock when you actually like just shoot your inflatomatic at an enemy where even if you've got a couple piled on top of each other, you should be able to get rid of them.

00:08:23.840 --> 00:08:34.460
So it really is just, it's putting a lot of focus on does it feel good to destroy the island and are you motivated enough by points to do it?

00:08:34.460 --> 00:09:00.140
Um, but I'd say that like when it comes to comparing this game to the original Dig Dug, uh, where you're sort of like creating a maze for yourself and the enemies to go through and really the only other environmental, uh, effects are like manipulating the rocks that fall down once you kind of get rid of the support below them.

00:09:00.140 --> 00:09:11.920
Um, and I'd say that while, uh, yeah, like there's, um, that, that game had probably, that was more focused on just inflating enemies.

00:09:11.920 --> 00:09:16.560
Cause like you could totally ignore the maze aspect and just do the same thing here.

00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:26.500
I think in Dig Dug 2, um, the fault lines take a bigger prominence than anything that you could do with the mazes in Dig Dug.

00:09:26.500 --> 00:09:27.940
That's a good point.

00:09:28.500 --> 00:09:30.380
I'm, I'm with you for the most part.

00:09:30.600 --> 00:09:41.860
I think Dig Dug 2 definitely allows for more customization and options in general in terms of, like you were saying, you mostly would focus on inflating the enemies in Dig Dug 1.

00:09:42.160 --> 00:09:44.980
And, you know, if you got a rock to land on them, great.

00:09:45.140 --> 00:09:48.960
But that was probably more luck based than, uh, this fault system.

00:09:48.960 --> 00:09:58.980
Um, however, I don't know, Dig Dug felt, Dig Dug 1 felt more claustrophobic and that you could screw yourself over if you were focusing on inflating a particular enemy.

00:09:59.200 --> 00:10:05.760
Especially with the way that they can, the enemies can burrow without creating, uh, new escape paths for you.

00:10:06.020 --> 00:10:07.720
And they could, uh, pop out that way.

00:10:07.900 --> 00:10:16.200
In this game, because the land masses are, are pretty large, especially as the game opens up more, uh, and tries to become more difficult with removing the land masses.

00:10:16.200 --> 00:10:17.320
They give you more land.

00:10:17.480 --> 00:10:21.620
There's a lot to, like, remove yourself from and not get trapped by enemies.

00:10:21.920 --> 00:10:28.100
So I feel like it's just a trade-off thing where the underground aspect was maybe more claustrophobic but simpler.

00:10:28.300 --> 00:10:33.460
And now this rewards players who, um, you know, who try harder.

00:10:33.620 --> 00:10:36.460
You don't have to try, I guess, is the whole, like, argument I'm making here.

00:10:36.520 --> 00:10:41.840
It's like, you don't have to try to be good at this game because of that stun lock and, and the inflation and everything.

00:10:41.840 --> 00:10:50.920
But it's not going to be satisfying to the, compared to the person who, like, tries to destroy the land masses and take care of multiple enemies at a time.

00:10:51.020 --> 00:10:54.780
I don't know. I would find the game very boring if I just inflated every enemy.

00:10:54.940 --> 00:11:01.960
Oh, yeah. There's, there's not enough, there's not enough to that to make it more than, like, a few minutes of enjoyment.

00:11:03.820 --> 00:11:06.020
And I guess that brings up a good question, Sean.

00:11:06.200 --> 00:11:12.000
Is there enough here to justify, uh, you know, is this a full game?

00:11:12.180 --> 00:11:20.840
Or is this falling into the usual pitfall of an arcade game where it was better when you put 25 cents in and got what you got and then you walked away from it?

00:11:21.640 --> 00:11:31.260
Yeah, I think it, like, as a, in relation to what I just said, where if it was just the inflation, you'd get, like, a few minutes of enjoyment out of it.

00:11:31.620 --> 00:11:39.080
This, with the fault lines, I give it, like, a good 20 to 30 minutes of enjoyment.

00:11:39.080 --> 00:11:42.320
Uh, it's definitely not Super Mario.

00:11:42.640 --> 00:11:51.100
It definitely doesn't have the same staying power as a game that isn't obviously designed for the arcade first.

00:11:51.500 --> 00:11:57.500
And they just needed some pretty obvious improvements in, uh, I don't know when Dig Dug 2 came out in arcades.

00:11:57.600 --> 00:12:00.820
I, uh, I want to say, like, 84 or something like that.

00:12:00.840 --> 00:12:07.500
But it just needed some obvious improvements for 1989 that I think would have really fixed this game for the better.

00:12:07.500 --> 00:12:12.660
Just, just think about how many enemies they could have also introduced per, maybe every 10 rounds, right?

00:12:12.760 --> 00:12:17.840
Like, now there's this new guy who's a little more complicated and, uh, he's actually immune to your inflation.

00:12:18.180 --> 00:12:20.120
Or, uh, he takes more pumps.

00:12:20.380 --> 00:12:21.420
Yeah, or he can jump, right.

00:12:21.600 --> 00:12:23.220
Jump, jumping's great, see?

00:12:23.360 --> 00:12:26.760
Little things like that, I think, would have, uh, would have improved it.

00:12:27.100 --> 00:12:33.700
Um, maybe even something to break up the rounds, uh, like a bonus, uh, thing since they're so focused on the high score.

00:12:33.700 --> 00:12:35.920
Or, uh, I'm kind of surprised that those things aren't there.

00:12:36.020 --> 00:12:37.540
But we've seen them in a lot of other games.

00:12:37.600 --> 00:12:46.440
I think that's what this is missing, is that it's a very straightforward game that, uh, kind of perfectly balances, uh, I think of it like a Kirby game.

00:12:46.620 --> 00:12:52.840
You know, where it's like, Kirby is easy enough that you can just float over all the enemies and you can just inflate all the enemies in Dig Dug.

00:12:52.840 --> 00:13:03.660
But if you wanted to also approach the game from a high score mechanics perspective, or in Kirby, if you wanted to play, like, the hard mode, uh, which is only unlocked after you beat the game usually.

00:13:03.960 --> 00:13:09.480
If you want to do that stuff, it's there, and it's fun for skilled players who already, like, got their fix.

00:13:09.660 --> 00:13:19.820
I feel like Dig Dug 2 kind of needed that, you know, not new game plus, but, like, after you complete all the rounds, a harder difficulty mode or something else.

00:13:19.820 --> 00:13:23.220
Like, now do it without being able to inflate enemies or something.

00:13:23.460 --> 00:13:24.920
Or it has charges or something.

00:13:25.160 --> 00:13:38.000
Right, right. But when we were talking earlier about, like, a learning curve, at least about how the fault lines work and the arrangements, like, that's really just if you're trying to play the game how it was designed.

00:13:38.220 --> 00:13:45.280
And, like, there really isn't too much of a learning curve because, like, you could just ignore them, like we were saying.

00:13:45.280 --> 00:13:59.780
Uh, I think, like, when, in terms of these levels, there really isn't too much of, like, like, if there was actually difficulty increasing, uh, that would be, like, a different game.

00:13:59.780 --> 00:14:03.080
Because really all that's happening in this one is the levels are different.

00:14:03.340 --> 00:14:06.480
Different arrangements, not entirely, like, harder arrangements.

00:14:07.080 --> 00:14:11.920
And it's not that I want them, because one of the ideas I think is suggested in the harder mode is to get rid of the inflation.

00:14:12.120 --> 00:14:15.180
It's not that the, uh, air pump is bad.

00:14:15.200 --> 00:14:18.660
Uh, I actually think that's, like, that's the thing that makes it uniquely Dig Dug.

00:14:18.700 --> 00:14:19.560
That's Dig Dug, yeah.

00:14:19.660 --> 00:14:30.440
I think it's the stun lock on the air pump that ruins the game because, like you said, that's the, that's the ability to just, like, hit every enemy that's around you, stun them really quick,

00:14:30.440 --> 00:14:39.520
and then remove yourself from the situation, uh, to gain more time, you know, it's like, if, if they just made it to this way, you had to commit to your pump,

00:14:39.660 --> 00:14:44.660
like, once you lock onto somebody, it's either pump them or, uh, or walk away.

00:14:44.820 --> 00:14:47.760
And who knows, maybe the stun lock would be, yeah, that's weird.

00:14:48.160 --> 00:14:56.140
Um, maybe the stun lock would be if how long you pump them, like, they have to then deflate to come back into battle.

00:14:56.140 --> 00:14:58.320
Maybe that's enough of a stun lock.

00:14:58.960 --> 00:15:06.580
Yeah, uh, because I know that you can just, you can continue, there's no, like, combo break if you take your attention away.

00:15:06.760 --> 00:15:14.840
They basically stay there and they'll only take another hit or two so that, like, you could just crowd control everyone.

00:15:14.840 --> 00:15:27.080
So, like, yeah, if, if it made it so that you, like, could only sort of, like, stun, but that wouldn't contribute to actually destroying, uh, maybe that would add some, some challenge.

00:15:30.700 --> 00:15:45.120
This is one of the few games that we've played with a level select feature, which I feel like all arcade games on the NES should have just came with at this point because they were probably so popular in the arcades that for most people, they didn't want to always have to start at level one again.

00:15:45.560 --> 00:15:50.560
Uh, it's pressing start, select, and the A button, uh, all at the title screen.

00:15:50.900 --> 00:15:55.700
Might take a few tries, though, because any one of those things also enters you into the game.

00:15:55.700 --> 00:16:02.300
So, if you accidentally don't press all three simultaneously, you run the risk of, uh, entering the game normally.

00:16:02.580 --> 00:16:04.940
So, uh, maybe they should have thought about that.

00:16:05.860 --> 00:16:09.400
I mean, did you choose to level select anything?

00:16:09.780 --> 00:16:11.300
No, I, I, yeah. I could care less.

00:16:12.220 --> 00:16:19.780
Yeah, uh, for me, the, you know, the round 31 wasn't like, oh, now Dig Dug 2 is getting somewhere, you know?

00:16:21.420 --> 00:16:22.940
I don't think I got there.

00:16:23.580 --> 00:16:26.480
But we've ran into that, I think, at least with Dig Dug 2.

00:16:26.820 --> 00:16:41.700
We've ran into a problem with, uh, I think it was way back, Wrecking Crew, where they did let you level select, uh, and I think they had 99 levels, and, like, the 99th level wasn't, like, particularly any more challenging than the early levels.

00:16:41.700 --> 00:16:45.460
It was just, like, another different way of doing a Wrecking Crew level.

00:16:46.040 --> 00:16:52.500
Uh, don't hold me to that, I'm not saying it's, like, as easy as level one, but it definitely wasn't, like, this, you know, now we put our hardest one.

00:16:52.680 --> 00:16:55.640
Same thing with, it was Bugs Bunny's Crazy Castle, if you remember.

00:16:56.100 --> 00:16:59.520
Which, uh, isn't the title of that game, it's THE Bugs Bunny's Crazy Castle.

00:16:59.680 --> 00:17:01.100
I like the first one better, but.

00:17:01.120 --> 00:17:15.960
Right. No, I know exactly what you mean, like, they're just different, they're not, they're not more difficult, there's just 32, whatever, however many levels there are, different levels, not, not harder levels.

00:17:16.900 --> 00:17:21.200
Yeah, and I actually don't know how many there are, but I'm gonna take a stab in the dark and say, like, 50.

00:17:21.400 --> 00:17:23.220
I don't feel like there were more than 50.

00:17:26.040 --> 00:17:34.820
On the sequels and spinoff side, it'd be interesting to note that, uh, there's probably a bunch of other Dig Dug games, and I think in the Bites episodes we were talking about the Mr.

00:17:35.000 --> 00:17:39.080
Driller series, which is kind of a, uh, spiritual sequel or whatever.

00:17:39.080 --> 00:17:45.680
But, um, today I want to talk about Dig Dug Digging Strike for the Nintendo DS.

00:17:46.180 --> 00:17:49.440
Is that, like, is that now he's a baseball player?

00:17:49.700 --> 00:17:55.860
See, I was, I was hoping you would actually get it, uh, but Digging Strike does imply baseball, maybe.

00:17:56.240 --> 00:18:08.840
Um, no, you see, Digging Strike is finally, like, again, somebody at the company, um, just had, like, they had an amazing idea where they were like, what if we take, and it's not exactly this, but follow it along.

00:18:09.080 --> 00:18:19.700
What if we took Dig Dug 2's top screen style and made that the top screen, and then the bottom screen is the Dig Dug 1 style gameplay?

00:18:20.080 --> 00:18:25.160
So, using the DS dual screen, you are, like, on the island, right?

00:18:25.500 --> 00:18:30.300
Wait, it's like, is it two, is it, it's like two planes making a three-dimensional game?

00:18:30.760 --> 00:18:34.960
Uh, almost, except for your character isn't on both at the same time, obviously.

00:18:34.960 --> 00:18:46.780
You can be, like, up on the island taking care of enemies up there and then dig a hole and go down into the, um, into the Dig Dug 1 style gameplay from the island.

00:18:46.980 --> 00:19:00.520
I feel like that is a pretty cool combination of the two games and is much better of a Dig Dug thing than, uh, what we, what we suggested this one might have been, where, like, this is the dugging part.

00:19:00.520 --> 00:19:03.040
Yeah. No, I, I, I like that.

00:19:03.180 --> 00:19:04.440
Did you, did you play it?

00:19:04.600 --> 00:19:08.200
I did, because I have a hacked 3DS, so I can do that.

00:19:08.720 --> 00:19:11.620
And, um, you know, I wouldn't, I wouldn't pay for this game.

00:19:11.800 --> 00:19:19.920
But I will say, uh, I think it made, I don't know, like, it made it interesting, right, as, like, a concept and everything.

00:19:19.920 --> 00:19:28.160
